# C#

Formatting is CSharpier's (`.cursor/rules/engineering.mdc`). This rule covers what a formatter cannot.

## Style

- Modern C# is allowed: `var` when the type is apparent on the right, collection expressions, target-typed `new`, primary constructors, file-scoped namespaces, `required` members, `ref struct`, `scoped`.
- Private fields are `_camelCase`, static ones included. Constants are PascalCase. No `this.` unless needed to disambiguate. Interfaces start with `I`.
- Namespaces follow assembly names, not folders. No type has the name of a namespace (CA1724 is an error).
- One type per file by default. A file name matches its main type.
- No `using` alias to shorten a BCL type name. Qualify the name where it collides.
- Library `await` uses `ConfigureAwait(false)`. `CancellationToken` is the last parameter.
- A new overload goes above the XML docs and attributes of the member it joins, not between them and that member.

## Types

Make an invalid state unrepresentable; do not document it away or catch it at run time.

- One choice, one slot: an enum, a sealed record hierarchy, or a generic parameter, not a `bool` plus a nullable that the `bool` makes meaningless.
- A value that has been verified has its own type (a verified license is not the same type as a parsed, unverified token), so unverified data cannot reach a decision.
- Identifiers are distinct types (`LicenseId`, `LeaseId`, `KeyId`), so one cannot be passed where another is expected. The `default` of an ID type never refers to a live value.
- A method that cannot fail returns its value, not a `bool`/`out` pair or a nullable.
- A public method does not throw because the caller used the wrong phase of the same type; split the type instead.
- The public surface is what the design lists. Everything else is `internal`.

## Errors

- An expected outcome that a caller decides on (a license that is expired, a seat that is not free, a signature that does not verify) is a return value with a reason the design lists, not an exception.
- Argument checks throw `ArgumentException` and its subtypes, with one-sentence English messages that name the condition.
- Public exception types are few and documented in the XML docs of the members that throw them.
- A `catch` that runs cleanup which can throw keeps the exception it caught: when the cleanup throws too, throw an `AggregateException` with the caught exception first, then the cleanup's.
- A comment that states how a runtime or OS API behaves names what was checked: the call, the version, and the result.
- A call that can fail by its result is checked (`TryParse`, `TryImport...`, `TryGetValue`).

## XML docs

- Public members under `src/` have `summary`, and `param` / `returns` / `exception` when a caller might be unsure. Sentences end with a period. Properties start with "Gets" / "Gets or sets"; constructors with "Initializes a new instance of the <see cref="T"/> class".
- XML docs state the caller's contract only. How it works lives in `docs/design.ja.md`; refer to the section instead of restating it.
- Use `<inheritdoc />` when an implementation has the interface's contract.
- Wording and terminology: `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`.
